# One job per (case, part), producing the SIXTEEN-BURST shard pool.
#
#   condor_submit condor/extract_multi.sub
#
# Reuses condor/cases.txt, so the (case, part, video) triples are exactly the
# ones the sparse pool was built from. That is deliberate: the dense shards
# have to hold the same windows as their sparse twins, or a 3D-vs-2D result
# measures the dataset instead of the architecture.
#
# Videos are NOT transferred -- HTCondor refuses to transfer out of /staging
# at all, and shipping a 2 GB video per job would be pointless when the
# staging mount makes it directly readable. +WantStagingMount gives the job
# that mount; the script reads the video and writes the shard in place.

universe                = container
container_image         = file:///staging/n/nkalthoff/surgvu26/surgvu26-extract.sif

executable              = condor/extract_multi16.sh
arguments               = $(case_id) $(part) $(video)

+WantStagingMount       = true

should_transfer_files   = YES
when_to_transfer_output = ON_EXIT
# `config` as a DIRECTORY: HTCondor flattens transferred file paths, so
# config/tool_frequency.json would arrive as bare tool_frequency.json and the
# script's path check would fail (it did, on the sparse run's first attempt).
transfer_input_files    = src, scripts, config

# 1 CPU and 4 GB, NOT the dense run's 2 and 8. This is a throughput fix, not
# a guess: counted against the live pool, 8GB/2cpu matches 735 slots and
# 4GB/1cpu matches 14,596 -- twenty times as many. The first submission of
# these 281 jobs sat with "2 slots match and are willing to run your job" and
# nothing started for ten minutes.
#
# The headroom is still real: the sparse run peaked at 2,887 MB on a full
# 160-window job, and this holds 32 frames per window against 30 while
# decoding them in four short runs rather than one, so the resident set is
# essentially unchanged. 1.2 GB of margin. If a job is HELD for memory,
# resubmit that one with memory=8GB rather than raising it for all 281.
request_cpus            = 1
request_disk            = 8GB

log                     = logs/multi16_$(case_id)_part$(part).log
error                   = logs/multi16_$(case_id)_part$(part).err
output                  = logs/multi16_$(case_id)_part$(part).out

# 46 of the 281 pairs legitimately hold no task segments and exit non-zero by
# design, so do not retry blindly. The staging-mount guard exits 75 and IS
# worth retrying, which is what the retry budget is for.
# SELF-HEALING MEMORY, so the next run of this pool does not need a human
# releasing jobs by hand. The 4 GB request is right for the overwhelming
# majority -- counted live, the pool holds 14,604 slots at >=4 GB and only
# 1,602 at >=6 GB, so the cliff is between those two and asking for 6 costs
# nine tenths of the cluster. The cases that cross it are the big ones: a
# 160-window case holds 160 x 32 frames of 512x512x3 in memory before
# write_shard encodes any of them, which is about 4.0 GB.
#
# HoldReasonCode 34 is "exceeded cgroup memory". Escalating on retry gives
# those cases 8 GB on their second attempt while everything else keeps the
# wide match. JobRunCount bounds it: a job that dies twice is a bug, not a
# big case, and should stay held where it can be seen.
# A PLAIN INTEGER, and the reason is worth keeping. The first attempt at
# self-healing was
#
#     request_memory = ifthenelse(JobRunCount > 0, 8192, 4096)
#
# and it took the cluster from 28 running to 5. JobRunCount is UNDEFINED at
# match time, so the ifthenelse evaluated to undefined, so
# `TARGET.Memory >= RequestMemory` was undefined, so it matched ZERO slots --
# condor_q -better-analyze reported "387 slots are rejected by your job's
# requirements" with no other diagnosis. An expression that references an
# attribute the machine-side context does not carry does not fail loudly; it
# silently matches nothing.
#
# Escalation therefore lives in condor/release_held.sh, which edits the
# memory on a HELD job -- where JobRunCount and MemoryUsage are both defined --
# and releases it. Less elegant, but it works, and it fails visibly.
request_memory   = $(memory:4096)

max_retries             = 2

queue case_id, part, video from condor/cases_dense_smoke.txt
